==English==
{{merge|kafir|fragment=kaffir and kafir}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|Caffer|caffre|kaffer|Kaffir|Kaffre|kafir|kaphar|kaphir|kafari}}

===Etymology===
Ultimately from {{der|en|ar|كَفَّار||infidel}} or {{m|ar|كَافِر||unbeliever}}, both from {{m|ar|كَفَرَ||to cover, to hide}}; in some (especially early) uses, via {{bor|en|es|cafre}}, {{bor|en|nl|kaffer}} or other European languages. {{doublet|en|kafir}}.
{{root|en|ar|ك ف ر}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkæfə/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-kaffir.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkæfɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|æfə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|offensive}} In Islamic contexts, a non-Muslim. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1804|author=Archibald Duncan|title=The Mariner's Chronicle|section=I
|passage=He {{...}} put me in imminent danger of my life, by telling the natives that I was a '''Caffer''', and not a Mussulman.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|offensive}} A member of the [[Nguni]] people of southern Africa, especially a [[Xhosa]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1792|title=The Analytical Review, Or History of Literature, Domestic and Foreign, on an Enlarged Plan|volume=14
|passage=… the Hambonaas, a nation quite different from the '''Kaffers''', having a yellowish complexion […].}}
# {{lb|en|countable|South Africa|Rhodesia|Namibia|ethnic slur|offensive|derogatory}} A [[black]] person. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1959|author=Alf Ross|title=On Law and Justice
|passage=If you ask a '''Kaffir''' why he does so-and-so, he will answer—"How can I tell? It has always been done by our forefathers."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1971|author=Naboth Mokgatle|title=The Autobiography of an Unknown South African
|passage=I once heard him say to the gardener, 'Come along, son.' His wife scolded him saying, 'He's not son, don't call him son, he's a '''kaffir'''.'}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1998|author=w:Antjie Krog|title=w:Country of My Skull
|passage="… and today here a white man is calling me a '''kaffir'''. This term that I absolutely resented." And that, says Nofomela, is his political motive.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=2000-04-09|author=Squirrel|title=SA Silence on Mugabe's actions|url=https://groups.google.com/forum/#!original/soc.culture.south-africa/w3PFP9oU9bg/FlXNVf7xoRwJ|newsgroup=soc.culture.south-africa|passage=Monkey see, monkey do ... which in this case refers to the baboons in Rhodesia - proves just how vulnerable the whities are in the hands of the '''kaffirs'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|now|historical|offensive}} A language spoken by the [[Nguni]] peoples of southern Africa, especially [[Xhosa]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1952|author=w:Doris Lessing|title=Martha Quest|publisher=Panther|year_published=1974|page=73
|passage=This man, seeing a white person enter, moved aside for her, but she saw Joss's eyes on her, and said in kitchen '''kaffir''', ‘No, when you've finished.’}}
# {{lb|en|finance|slang|historical}} South African mining shares {{defdate|from early 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1907|journal=[[w:Truth (British periodical)|''Truth'']]|volume=62|page=688
|passage='''Kaffirs''' bouyant most of last week}}
# {{senseid|en|kaffir corn}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} {{ellipsis of|en|kaffir corn}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1907|author=Texas Agricultural; Mechanical College System|journal=Bulletin|volume=93-117|page=5
|passage=This market reports only one or two cars per day, selling by the hundred weight, and at a price a little lower than that of Indian corn. As to the purpose for which the marketed '''kaffir''' is used, there is some uncertainty.}}

====Usage notes====
This word was widely used in the nineteenth and early twentieth century. Since the mid-twentieth century it has been regarded as derogatory.

